Z||000017||ChopTop||12-04-2001||08:08 AM||||I may be interested but... I am currently running Suspension Techniques lowering springs all around.  I'm happy with the way the front looks, but the rear still sits approximately an inch to an inch and a half too high for my taste.  I seem to remember reading here that the coilovers sit no lower than ST lowering springs.  If that's true it wouldn't make any sense for me to get in on this.  From the pic that is posted of these it appears they are not adjustable?  ||||reg||1
Z||000018||cadero2dmax||12-04-2001||08:35 AM||||One of the major benefits to the adjustable perch "coil-over" struts is the fact that any speedshop has the 2 1/2" springs. You can lower these struts by getting shorter springs, starting at around $40 or $50. I had 4 or 5 sets of springs for my racer, all with different spring values, for tuning the suspension to different surfaces/temps/etc. <P>If you want to lower the car as low as my Q4 racecar was (1/2" off the ground at the drivers door with a STIFF suspension - see the current "Anybody Autocross These Things"  thread for pix) you can get 8" 350lb springs. <P>For a stock height to 2" lowering job, the kit usually comes with 12" springs at 250#. For 2 - 4" , a 10 inch spring is what is needed(this is what I would recommend for the street, and I would suggest a 275 to 300lb spring with this setup).<P>BTW, the increased cost for the '88 cradle is simply because you need strut adapters to make this swap, and that additional cost is for those adapters.<P>Wouldn't this topic be better suited to the "Mall" forum?<P>------------------<BR>G<P>Daily Driver - <BR>85 SE<BR>4.9 Cad<BR>Isuzu 5 speed<BR>'88 cradle<BR>lowered 2 1/2"<BR>Mr. Mikes interior<P>GRM $2002 Challenge Fiero -<BR>86 coupe<BR>4.9 (carburated)<BR>2.86:1 TH-125 w/shift kit<BR>'88 cradle<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by cadero2dmax (edited 12-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||Nashco||12-04-2001||09:25 AM||||ChopTop, it's exactly like cadero2dmax said.  That's the beauty of the coilovers is that they are fully adjustable for exactly what you need them to do.  You can get different length springs and different spring rates, then adjust accordingly with the threaded sleeves on the coil overs.<P>1s - 2<BR>2s - 1<BR>3s - 3<BR>4s - 3<P>The mall?  Perhaps it should belong there; I anticipated a bunch of questions and discussion along with the topic, and thought it would be best if it was archived and searchable later on.  Yes no?<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000020||cadero2dmax||12-04-2001||10:31 AM||||Bryce<P>By the time this hits the archives, the sale will be over.<P>OTOH, the conversation/topic might be of interest for some time to come, I guess.<P>Rock on, Dude!!<P>G<P><p>[This message has been edited by cadero2dmax (edited 12-04-2001).]||||reg||1
