Z||000052||Nashco||12-10-2001||11:33 AM||||The only adjustable struts we can get for the rear are the Konis, but at twice the price, you definitely pay for performance!  The KYBs are only adjustable as far as the spring size, rate, positioning, etc....good enough for me at my financial position.  I'm not even a good enough tuner yet to be able to use the Konis to their full abilities.<P>1s - 5<BR>2s - 2<BR>3s - 10<BR>4s - 2<P>I hear ya on the timing, luckily I already got Christmas gifts a long time ago, and I'm expecting a little bonus income from some recently sold parts.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000053||DKOV||12-10-2001||11:55 AM||||KYBs are "self adjusting".  At least, that is their claim to fame.<P>The concept is that the KYB shocks "sense" when normal driving changes to performance driving.  So, when the car is lazily putting along, a nice smooth ride is experienced.  When the nose takes a sharp dive under hard braking, the shocks respond by increasing the dampening resistance.<P>Don't ask me exactly how it works but it seems like a dual stage piston with a velocity actuator.<P>Anyway, it is a very GOOD setup and many, many cars & trucks use them.  The other benefit is that you can use them effectively for BOTH daily and track driving without getting the car off the ground and adjusting settings by trial and effort.<P>The biggest benefit is that you can tootle around town and then, when disaster strikes, the car can instantly perform as needed.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I tend to race my car more than drive it, so the Koni Adjustables were my choice, but I did have the KYBs on there before and I really liked them.  I highly recommend them.<P>DKOV -||||reg||1
